## Environment

This project runs inside a VS Code devcontainer. Claude Code, its own dependencies, and all
project tooling (linters, test runner, database clients) live inside the container. The
container:

- Can only see this project's files, plus its own persistent Claude Code config at
  `~/.claude` (bind-mounted from `.devcontainer/claude-home/`, gitignored). It has no access
  to any other part of the host filesystem.
- Sees `.devcontainer/` as **read-only** (the container that defines the sandbox cannot be
  rewritten from inside it). To change the devcontainer, Dockerfile, or `post-create.sh`,
  edit them on the host and rebuild — those edits cannot be made from within the container.
- Runs as an ordinary, **unprivileged** container: no host block devices, no host Docker
  socket, no host filesystem beyond this workspace.
{% if firewall_enabled %}
- Has restricted **network egress**: `/usr/local/bin/cdforge-firewall` (rendered from
  `.devcontainer/init-firewall.sh`) runs on every container start and rejects everything
  except DNS, this container's own networks, and an allowlist of hosts — the host itself,
  reachable at the Docker bridge gateway on any other devcontainer, is rejected. If a network
  call fails with "connection refused" to a host that should work, that is the firewall, not
  the code: the allowlist is extended on the host (`.devcontainer/firewall-allow.txt`), which
  you cannot do from in here.
{% if network_firewall == 'strict' %}
- Has **no passwordless sudo**: `sudo` works only for the firewall script. Do not try to
  install system packages — they belong in `.devcontainer/Dockerfile`, edited on the host.
{% endif %}
{% else %}
- Is **not** network-isolated: it can reach the internet, the host's LAN, and the host itself
  through the Docker bridge gateway. Only the filesystem is sandboxed. Treat anything you send
  over the network as leaving the sandbox.
{% endif %}
{% if needs_service_stack %}
- Provides its backing services ({% if db_is_server %}the {{ database }} database{% endif %}{% if db_is_server and include_celery %} and {% endif %}{% if include_celery %}Redis{% endif %}) through `docker-compose.yml`, which you run
  yourself from inside the container (`docker compose up -d`) using the in-container Docker
  daemon. Those containers stay inside the sandbox and are reached at `localhost`.
{% endif %}
{% if docker_mode == 'sysbox' %}
- Has an in-container Docker daemon isolated by the Sysbox runtime. You can `docker
  build`/`docker run`/use Testcontainers here; the container is still unprivileged and has no
  host access. If `docker` is not yet responding after a restart, run
  `bash .devcontainer/docker-start.sh`.
{% endif %}
{% if enable_docker %}
- Has in-container Docker (Docker-in-Docker) enabled. NOTE: this makes the container run
  `--privileged`, which grants access to the host kernel and block devices and therefore
  breaks the host-isolation guarantee above. Treat host resources as reachable when this is
  on. The daemon is started by `.devcontainer/docker-start.sh`; if `docker` is not
  responding after a restart, run `bash .devcontainer/docker-start.sh`.
{% endif %}
{% if gpu_enabled %}
- Has GPU access for processes running directly inside the devcontainer (`--gpus=all`).
{% endif %}
- Starts every terminal session in Claude Code's `auto` permission mode, so commands run
  without interactive approval prompts. `git push` is discouraged at multiple layers (a
  `permissions.deny` entry plus a `.githooks/pre-push` hook that refuses pushes from inside
  the devcontainer — it keys off the `CDFORGE_DEVCONTAINER` env var, so the same hook stays
  out of the way when a human pushes from the host); the real reason it cannot push is that
  no push credentials are mounted into the container.

## Generated, managed files

`.devcontainer/`, `.githooks/` and `.claude/` were generated by `cdforge` from the answers
recorded in `.cdforge.json`, and a host-side `cdforge adopt` re-renders them from the
template. Improvements to them therefore belong upstream in cdforge; a local edit here is
lost on the next alignment run (and `.devcontainer/` is read-only from inside the container
anyway). Everything else in this repository — {% if stack == 'static' %}the site's HTML, CSS and JavaScript{% elif stack == 'node' %}source code, tests, `package.json`{% else %}source code, tests, `pyproject.toml`{% endif %}, this
file, `README.md`, `CHANGELOG.md` — belongs to the project and is never rewritten by
`cdforge adopt`.

## Language and style

- All code, comments, commit messages, and documentation are in English.
{% if stack == 'node' %}
- Dependencies are managed with `npm`; run project commands through the `package.json`
  scripts (`npm start`, `npm run build`, `npm test`, `npm run lint`) or `npx <tool>`
  rather than a global install. Never `pip install` anything — this is a Node project.
- Code style is enforced by ESLint + Prettier (`npm run lint`):
  - String literals use single quotes (Prettier `singleQuote`).
  - Components are standalone (no NgModules); prefer the Angular CLI (`npx ng generate`)
    to create components, services and routes so the conventions stay consistent.
{% elif stack == 'static' %}
- This is a plain static website: hand-written HTML, CSS and JavaScript served exactly as
  they are on disk. There is **no build step, bundler, framework or package manager** — do
  not add one. Reference every asset with a relative path so the site works from any
  sub-path.
- Preview it from the repository root with `python3 -m http.server 8000` and open the
  forwarded port; there is nothing to install first.
- Keep the markup valid and semantic and check the pages you touched in a browser before
  committing.
{% else %}
- Dependencies and virtual environments are managed with `uv`; run project commands with
  `uv run ...` rather than invoking tools from a global install.
- Code style is enforced by `ruff` (`uv run ruff check .`, `uv run ruff format .`):
  - String literals use single quotes.
  - Imports are one per line and sorted (`ruff` `isort` rules with
    `force-single-line = true`); never combine multiple names in one `from x import a, b`.
{% endif %}

